#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128955 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40733109-8985-5f30-9678-289216c1da00" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "75d559bd-99c1-5374-9ae6-0096a8b1d86e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5806" :question/problem "Sketch the curve $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}(x-4)(x^2 + 5).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 5 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 0^2 - 4(1)(5) = -20 < 0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(0-4)({0}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-4)(5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8d12adf0f04b7c1d9d05271c9378b941cf107d713932055a1fc48371952a1c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128956 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "1ebf167f-0c61-5721-9019-1da86d591c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5808" :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 4x - 24,$ sketch the curve $y = f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & -4 & -24 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 24 \\\\  & 1 & 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f(x)}{x-3} = x^2 + 4x + 8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x^2 + 4x + 8).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 4x + 8 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 4^2 - 4(1)(8) = -16 <                    0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-4(0)-24=-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-24),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/fa/fa1af56b5acbb196567d5943db53cf34405ab59cdb5e73a8a9fc8021d2864373.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "acf8ac05-4080-50ea-8a33-dca4a08cb7e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5810" :question/problem "Sketch the curve $f(x) = -(x-2)(x^2 + 2x + 2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 2x + 2 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 2^2 - 4(1)(2) = -4 <  0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)({0}^{2}+2(0)+2) \\\\  & =-(-2)(2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/1b/1bbbfefc0260959a5008b5b4597293e0d4acdeb390601cfdb53a8d700ffbbf54.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "50dda1cf-e61a-5efb-9671-a66d71360b84" :question/math-academy-id "e-5813" :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x) = -x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + 16,$ sketch the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & -4 & 16 \\\\  &  & -4 & 0 & -16 \\\\  & -1 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = -(x-4)(x^2 + 4).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 4 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0^2 - 4(1)(4) = - 16  <     0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny = -(0)^3 + 4(0)^2 - 4(0) + 16 = 16\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,16),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/aa/aa0418567de10fa6bd229a3d1bfbff2a71f242232f8af8dd001b096ecc9c8038.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40733109-8985-5f30-9678-289216c1da00" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2abd7b01-78b3-5242-a601-d2ef07b80ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8a5e889a374aae31d7cf7dd82581b00c4d74abfc08b14721f1da3ea19b2e95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557958} {:answer/id #uuid "6a731023-f149-5d7e-8f43-28d7661afe31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d322525e8872269fae28165bfacd7babc83a19fda176fd9940a852466d79fb04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558070} {:answer/id #uuid "77dcd354-9b84-55c2-af06-8f1ec192e3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a5fbc1a0714085042ca131d298f86a064aec35657e0fead5c67b6029d90b3cc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558101} {:answer/id #uuid "8acebe57-509e-5cb0-ae9e-c8309c5783f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b084ee964d74c0c57e9ae91d3feacf80f19529b811f802ef2a3e724e6abbe092.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558139} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cb9a87-d708-50b6-a39e-33a416ff452a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8cf40e338097e3686a17b553aa225e791fdb2257dd1a479885139dec6d3a9fd6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2abd7b01-78b3-5242-a601-d2ef07b80ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8a5e889a374aae31d7cf7dd82581b00c4d74abfc08b14721f1da3ea19b2e95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557958} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b83f475-f63f-50c5-aa47-1f832d4a1605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f891d27b-7abc-5935-b2a7-003a7da88708" :question/math-academy-id "q-112427" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+1)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(3(0)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(4)(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/fa8a5e889a374aae31d7cf7dd82581b00c4d74abfc08b14721f1da3ea19b2e95.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66fa054a-3434-5832-a6d3-05c9bf94cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/633941510daf5f1edb6566ee416767d303e53e7aa15325087929b91cdefb94f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558066} {:answer/id #uuid "bcfd3779-a56b-5dc0-bbd4-92df91a639ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b066c8462d80e759850baf1ac4b4011073f5a866e9d5eff47e06c4a3ec8b63ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558244} {:answer/id #uuid "c14e9c27-ee53-58ac-bc1f-538c1b3160ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f9bcde0b753f45a491cbf378128d1523c0a40c7fafd2d6a182b28d0d55aced91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558255} {:answer/id #uuid "dd885a19-30b9-5fa7-8234-2b884c2e081c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7a04ed5e26758551d7eca9949a274884f28f43a001439b58e393470721d96e5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558306} {:answer/id #uuid "df1fc0a1-3c75-5121-a158-2789988b928d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9bd03d441a7c5acfef36d8d8534da911c27f8c78387eddb7717371ae90d01d16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd885a19-30b9-5fa7-8234-2b884c2e081c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7a04ed5e26758551d7eca9949a274884f28f43a001439b58e393470721d96e5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558306} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fab39b0-c0b6-5581-852e-d6af4250c9fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e99018d-106a-536c-a32e-4bcfd5c27a01" :question/math-academy-id "q-112435" :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -3 \\\\  &  & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)(3{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(0)^{3}-9(0)^{2}+0-3=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a04ed5e26758551d7eca9949a274884f28f43a001439b58e393470721d96e5d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1851c0-2e94-5cbb-9238-911d535aad6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b5555c7c291b522600a2afd422fd5ba79d9ede51c2392057d7ed79977386e040.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557930} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7285b2-42c9-5a72-b2d5-5c262ae4ea3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/883cb4c02844df9c8ae0db49aa2db235d1e71da262420ccf570ca9acdcfbf2e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557933} {:answer/id #uuid "83bbbbb6-eb3f-52b8-b9ce-ba99291b2891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/13f9151db533e2ec997a5b2eef5ee49eaa8dea9d1b37f7f0cc853434f1360ca3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558121} {:answer/id #uuid "c114a863-3a02-52d0-9af8-557179f1db73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5eebba3872aa018aa6a2058d84e3a3ea90837b670ac15fd49f1d083496efe2ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558253} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d2ae1-97a4-550d-9639-0b5d74bc2af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1d05449fcf2c39749736b58099a32f59ff85566d5ea56d0e4ec61e88da8ce7aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79d2ae1-97a4-550d-9639-0b5d74bc2af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1d05449fcf2c39749736b58099a32f59ff85566d5ea56d0e4ec61e88da8ce7aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558329} :answer-field/id #uuid "d704154a-6d5d-5adf-9ea8-05adad19a091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07788a4c-c93b-51a6-bd5f-2540eb30230f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112498" :question/problem "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x-9,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -4 & -12 & -3 & -9 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & 9 \\\\  & -4 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-4{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(4{x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $4{x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(4)(3)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-4(0)^{3}-12(0)^{2}-3(0)-9=-9\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d05449fcf2c39749736b58099a32f59ff85566d5ea56d0e4ec61e88da8ce7aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b16f1085-6b45-5b90-8c6d-7f7be91cd536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/542195ea9617c2d674bb5a96a116033db8204114a5c8ba44a164c82b6133c552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126918} {:answer/id #uuid "10f104f2-cc54-5f5c-a53b-614166d0293b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03871a7ce58953f8cca6239641e0b18b7a4fcaed50fbed781c097ce2acbc2f08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126919} {:answer/id #uuid "119a15c5-2653-58d3-9dfe-83a5f6f95715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fe937dda01135a13db51ef5a3b5f924f0e53aab59e4fe583a2a55bac753d995.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126920} {:answer/id #uuid "17439721-eac2-5a3e-89b7-5307977ce0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126921} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1e691e-5f3e-549d-b797-a753fd439226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3df55302db431e7e2bc937028b1737956ae73299b59cf07be8e7b2fa02d2b87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17439721-eac2-5a3e-89b7-5307977ce0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126921} :answer-field/id #uuid "69675294-a915-57a9-baa8-613457e1b1e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4af252a3-6966-5006-8a08-9a500d4173b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-28196" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+3x+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(3)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-3<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)({0}^{2}+3(0)+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8ab678-e96f-5ebb-9983-b70a82dda722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed54bc47e32b1bd7cef59653a3c37b3d4fac0dc3e806b8a8aab07087b19e5890.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557896} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf4e2a9-4940-50d9-ae18-6efd87d160a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46ebde8dcd681cd48ff30dfc839bab3c5176496632398e5c3e530be61389603e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558045} {:answer/id #uuid "800ff475-823f-520a-bb9a-7ed63df0be1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/795ec06d95058de55d870db3a7559f44277003afdfd5ce1231493abd520404e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558115} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7ac8e1-e340-5006-a03e-b80b3ee17727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76e55d2573b66e07b0b8f927bb5dc1a4e7210f71cd979ebedc9b13e044311d21.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558167} {:answer/id #uuid "b70256e0-11d0-59c2-83f5-acf63d9cbb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5ac8512632c0609e6fe19a3bba8d17d3b665c7d3fd1b96135c9f0dc23c170526.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "800ff475-823f-520a-bb9a-7ed63df0be1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/795ec06d95058de55d870db3a7559f44277003afdfd5ce1231493abd520404e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558115} :answer-field/id #uuid "533637cd-4954-5318-b1fb-8100971c8607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99119256-67b3-5344-8812-e11eceeb5da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-28197" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/79/795ec06d95058de55d870db3a7559f44277003afdfd5ce1231493abd520404e2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e100e5-9655-5afe-9d8d-3f3667556e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19a62466d622c23d874043f565efa9a1339b8c3c1e8ccd9b10e1816c02bbd1f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557906} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc5ea52-a79c-59fd-9c64-5b4e4acf2345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e030ee4d5762ea111005d7adda728938fbdee59b2c95540ed3122042b40f9b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557990} {:answer/id #uuid "41324b3f-3759-54c1-9d02-556c07fe636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/00a7820952a0c243ce3f3c03915a1cdc17c02fa0f49e52d3acf6885cae2c4d12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557998} {:answer/id #uuid "955b475b-a661-58ae-a357-e025ab522fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83b4e298dce636116b0eae382640af0802f7632865092c52495eb5921e13e273.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558157} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e2affa-0916-50f7-a8bb-a628a95d474e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d391911307bc35f7481fe8b3a0b3871d1c4b75cc44d4459018fe00b14133f4f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e2affa-0916-50f7-a8bb-a628a95d474e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d391911307bc35f7481fe8b3a0b3871d1c4b75cc44d4459018fe00b14133f4f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558268} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe85277-a616-5cbf-990b-1747df437c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b580f52d-26f6-5405-9c2b-c27d4700d6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39094" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -5 & -3 \\\\  &  & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\  & -2 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-2{x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{2}+2x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)(2{x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(3)=-20<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-2(0)^{3}-4(0)^{2}-5(0)-3=-3\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d391911307bc35f7481fe8b3a0b3871d1c4b75cc44d4459018fe00b14133f4f9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dfd8d40-ee79-5c13-999a-273c61ccfd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a11984f9a9de7e220103525a3c925dee83a9eed37c4901f46046de7e80bc3a7f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324337} {:answer/id #uuid "46e897b5-bae2-55bb-a504-26d099cf0345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/585b326719cd74e9a7b26892df49742083ccafeea83379a517552f78be83e5ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324542} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecac441-46f0-5eeb-a26a-0dff106b6759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/98705a867d25e4f8991c29e9cda964b694c678cb1cb2de638f4d1d2f1ae37200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324617} {:answer/id #uuid "7da9e0e7-1b33-585d-8215-ce739e8fcb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325030} {:answer/id #uuid "93f8f1b0-6195-5f5a-a9e9-03cc49116f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/851b996ce1dab2e5777b5c7475785863009c0b8519b2805da72cbb4c07afcb47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da9e0e7-1b33-585d-8215-ce739e8fcb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325030} :answer-field/id #uuid "a692b341-ff58-5a69-9290-ce953c4bcc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f032169-ef22-5e6b-92e9-31b38bfaf554" :question/math-academy-id "q-39135" :question/problem "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40733109-8985-5f30-9678-289216c1da00" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2894aa1d-09bb-5dce-af9d-4f6e14c812e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/310ba3049a0a91f23259a67eb3c0606749f6a83a75151d6b0d41dfc4e98706d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557950} {:answer/id #uuid "2a037655-9b65-5ebf-b832-c87bb6130feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90bbdf52bb7854ec9746993c9ceca5e2d27b98262b3c6ddf2d194cfeeaeb4ee3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557954} {:answer/id #uuid "399801ab-4891-5a95-8c97-e166e87d8821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a37d7d9d81585f7fbe688d746d42974c4331f6468150761d0f2ef9333a37554.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557984} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8f63d2-617a-5b48-a0bf-425f5f16c222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf9765f57a5519c3d021251483cd3a97babe83d49712052d4e881ff9ecdb5f74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558146} {:answer/id #uuid "c11b8b6b-1872-5d17-b8bb-6611bdc0f4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/0852c1dbab24e5d176c9982e9c5d46f829cae9428022cc9c3b934206747b1cc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2894aa1d-09bb-5dce-af9d-4f6e14c812e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/310ba3049a0a91f23259a67eb3c0606749f6a83a75151d6b0d41dfc4e98706d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557950} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f04ac0d-7b8b-5f4e-b670-ef5a0deac443" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1114090a-5268-5e5c-ab01-061822ccfb1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75187" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)({0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =(-4)(3) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/31/310ba3049a0a91f23259a67eb3c0606749f6a83a75151d6b0d41dfc4e98706d1.png)"}]}
